      "solution": "import string\n\nmessage = input()\nresult = ''\nupper = prev = False\nfor c in message:\n    case = c in string.ascii_uppercase\n    if case and not prev: upper = not upper\n    if c != ' ': prev = case\n    result += c.upper() if upper else c.lower()\nprint(result)",
      "statement": "You were writing a message, but you accidentally used <<Caps Lock>> instead of <<Shift>>. Given the original message, print the string that was actually typed in. \n\nWe assume that for each group of consecutive uppercase letters and spaces, <<Shift>> (or, actually, <<Caps Lock>>) was pressed only once for the whole group. We also assume that <<Caps Lock>> both activates and deactivates when the key is pressed, and not when it is released.",
